ZC Land: draw the web player's canvas at its displayed size in device pixels.

Upstream sizes the web backbuffer to the virtual screen (640x480) unless a CRT filter is on, and CSS
stretches the canvas by a fractional factor (min(innerWidth/640, height/480)) with image-rendering:
pixelated. The game sits in that 640x480 at a whole 2x, so on screen each game pixel is 2 x that
factor device pixels - at 1.6x, 3.2, rounded into columns 3 and 4 wide. Almost no window size gives
a whole number, so game pixels come out uneven at nearly every size, in a pane and in full screen.

With this, the backbuffer always follows the canvas's displayed size in device pixels (bufW/bufH
from window.zcCanvasSize), filter or not, and main.js sizes the canvas box in device pixels so it
maps 1:1 at any devicePixelRatio. configure_render_tree then scales the game by a whole number
(scaling_force_integer, on by default) and letterboxes it. The rule for a page smaller than the
virtual screen is unchanged: the backbuffer never goes below 640x480, and a CRT filter turns off.

Applied by .github/workflows/zcl-web-player.yml to upstream's checkout before building (git apply).
If upstream changes this code, the apply fails and the build stops; refresh the patch.

